Reverse-engineering skill: Ghidra headless, with the output made readable
==========================================================================

Ghidra ships on this machine and its headless analyser works, so the missing piece was not capability
— it was that `analyzeHeadless` output is unusable as it stands. Every line of a script's output
arrives prefixed with `INFO  <Script>.java>`, multi-line strings lose the prefix after the first line
and gain a ` (GhidraScript)` tail, and the whole thing is interleaved with JVM warnings and ELF
relocation complaints. Reading that as decompiled C is how you end up quoting noise.

So the skill ships three files:

- `SKILL.md` — when to use this, what decompiled C can and cannot be trusted for, and the order of
  attack (strings first, then walk outward from a hit — not "dump everything").
- `DumpDecomp.java` — a Ghidra post-script that decompiles functions in address order, skips thunks,
  and stops at a function limit so a big binary does not produce a wall of text.
- `ghidra_dump.py` — the wrapper: runs `analyzeHeadless`, recovers exactly the script's payload from
  both echo forms, drops everything else, and exits non-zero when no real output was produced instead
  of printing an empty success.

Two things were found by running it rather than reading about it:

1. Ghidra refuses any project path with a component starting with `.` — `Path element starting with '.'
   is not permitted`. Sandboxed TMPDIRs contain one, so the wrapper picks a scratch directory with no
   such component and falls back to /tmp.
2. Java 27 throws a wall of `sun.misc.Unsafe` warnings at Ghidra 12.1.2. They are warnings: analysis
   completes normally.

Verified by decompiling /bin/true end to end: three functions of readable C, no Ghidra noise, exit 0.

